1. ONE LIST ON THE CLASS, SEEN THROUGH EVERY INSTANCE
     class Team:
         members = []
         def join(self, name):
             self.members.append(name)
     a, b = Team(), Team()
     a.join('ada')
     b.members                        ['ada']            nobody joined b: the list belongs to the class
     a.members is Team.members        True
     vars(a)                          {}                 the instance holds nothing of its own

2. A READ FALLS THROUGH TO THE CLASS; A WRITE DOES NOT
     class Counter:
         count = 0
     c = Counter()
     c.count                          0                  not on c, so found on the class
     c.count = 1
     Counter.count                    0                  the write went to c, not through to the class
     vars(c)                          {'count': 1}
     c.count                          1                  and now the read stops at c
     del c.count
     c.count                          0                  delete c's own, and the class's shows again
     c.count += 1
     (Counter.count, vars(c))         (0, {'count': 1})  += reads through, then writes to c

3. THE FIX: MAKE THE OBJECT IN __init__
     class Team:
         def __init__(self):
             self.members = []
         def join(self, name):
             self.members.append(name)
     a, b = Team(), Team()
     a.join('ada')
     b.members                        []                 each __init__ ran self.members = [] once, for its own instance
     a.members is b.members           False

4. WHEN SHARING IS THE POINT
     class Node:
         made = 0
         def __init__(self):
             Node.made += 1
     for _ in range(3): Node()
     Node.made                        3                  written through the class name, so it is one counter
     vars(Node())                     {}                 and no instance ever gets its own copy

5. A DATACLASS FIELD IS PER INSTANCE; AN UNANNOTATED NAME IS NOT
     @dataclass                       ValueError
     class Bag:
         items: list = []
     @dataclass
     class Bag:
         items = []
     x, y = Bag(), Bag()
     x.items.append(1)
     y.items                          [1]                no annotation, so not a field: a plain class attribute
     fields(Bag)                      ()                 the dataclass machinery never saw it

6. __slots__: A NAMESPACE THAT IS NOT A DICT
     class P:
         __slots__ = ('x',)
     p = P()
     p.x = 1
     p.y = 2                          AttributeError
     vars(p)                          TypeError          no __dict__ to hold a stray attribute
     class Q:                         ValueError
         __slots__ = ('x',)
         x = 0
